In article <1993Dec8.185046.27584@sarah.albany.edu> mg6365@albnyvms.bitnet writes:
>Again I wish to thank all to responding to my post of where I can I find Net  Hack for the Pc (which is located at linc.cis.upenn.edu).  I've been playing    it for a few days now (on and off) and I have what I would think the ultimate   newbie question which is...
>  What do the different classes do and the basic pro/cons of the classes?
>Thank you!

This is deliberately vague;  I don't want to give too much away.

Archaeologist:  Starts with a tinning kit, which can be very useful
later in the game, and the ability to identify gems (which can keep
you from lugging around 200 pieces of glass).  Otherwise a VERY
difficult character.

Barbarian:  Basic Hack'n'slasher.  Good starting weapon;  good
strength.  Pretty bad everything else, but it can survive early
combats well enough to make a good beginner character.

Caveman:  Sorta like a barbarian, but with neolithic weaponry.  Pretty
tough, IMO.

Elf:  Lots of starting intrinsic resistances.

Healer:  Lots of money, potions of healing, and just about nothing
else.  Not an easy class.

Knight:  Good armor and starting weaponry, and a chance for very good
weaponry later on.  Their poor statistics, though, make them generally
difficult to play.

Priest:  Able to tell when items are blessed or cursed.  This can help
you out a lot, especially at low levels.

Rogue:  Very difficult.  About all that I can say;  I don't play them.

Samurai:  Don't play these much.  Fairly good beginner characters, I
belive.  Later in the game, they have a chance for one of the game's
most powerful items.

Tourist:  Not for the faint of heart!  Play a complete newbie to the
dungeon, complete with Hawaiian shirt and darts.  If you can get far
enough into the game, you can get a powerful item to compensate you.

Valkyrie: Good strength.  Choose a female name;  the game forces you
to play female, and it will be odd having "Bill the Valkyrie"
called "Madam" in shops.  Decent starting weapon, although not as good
as the Barbarian's.  Valks have a real ace up their sleeve though,
which I'll leave it to you to find out.  Good starting characters.

Wizard:  Tough to play unless you know a couple of spoilers;  then
they become much easier.  Good starting spell ability, of course.
Probably not a good choice for beginners.

